    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Lab containers Zurich", "item": "https://planexus.de/en/lab-containers-zurich" }
  ]
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long does it take to deliver a lab container from the Albstadt works to Zurich?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The heavy-haulage route from the Albstadt works to Zurich runs south on the A81 via Singen, through the Thayngen/Schaffhausen border crossing with customs clearance, then the A4 to Winterthur and the A1 to Zurich. Driving time is around 2.5 hours over approximately 150 kilometres. Customs clearance at the Thayngen border (Swiss frontier) is handled under the T1 transit procedure with an ATA Carnet or direct import clearance — we co-ordinate the entire process. Standard delivery points are the Zurich-Nord (A1) junction for Hönggerberg and Irchel, Zurich-Brunau (A3) for the Rämistrasse hill, and Zurich-City for Hardstrasse. From order to operational handover in Zurich takes 8 to 14 weeks."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which permits do I need for a lab container in the Canton of Zurich?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The governing instruments are the Planungs- und Baugesetz (PBG, Planning and Building Act of the Canton of Zurich), the Allgemeine Bauverordnung (ABV, General Building Ordinance) and the City of Zurich’s Bau- und Zonenordnung (BZO, Building and Zoning Ordinance). Containers are treated as buildings and installations; a building permit (Baubewilligung) is required in most cases. For the City of Zurich the competent authority is the Amt für Baubewilligungen (Building Permit Office) at Lindenhofstrasse 19, 8001 Zurich. Temporary siting of under three months may, depending on the site, be possible under a simplified notification. For GMP, BSL-2 and BSL-3 containers, the Einschliessungsverordnung (ESV, Containment Ordinance) for genetically modified installations and the Heilmittelgesetz (HMG, Therapeutic Products Act) with Swissmedic competence also apply. We classify your site in advance and submit procedure-ready building documents for city and canton."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which seismic requirements apply to lab containers in Zurich under SIA 261?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Under SIA 261, Zurich lies in seismic zone Z1a — the lowest seismic zone in Switzerland. By comparison, Valais, Basel and the Engadin lie in Z2 to Z3b. For Z1a the horizontal design ground acceleration is 0.6 m/s² (agd). Container laboratories are usually classified in Bauwerksklasse I (BWK I, ordinary buildings); BSL-3 containers and pharmaceutical pilot plants may, depending on risk, be raised to BWK II, which requires higher safety factors. Planexus supplies containers for Zurich that are structurally verified to SIA 261, with an anchoring concept to SIA 263 (steel construction) from the Albstadt works — not DIN alone, because DIN structural calculations by themselves are not accepted in the Swiss permit procedure."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do customs clearance and VAT work for a lab container entering Switzerland?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Switzerland is not part of the EU Customs Union. We clear goods under the T1 transit procedure from the Albstadt works, with processing at the Thayngen (CH) or Schaffhausen customs office. Permanent import attracts Swiss VAT of 8.1 per cent (as at 2026). Temporary import (for example a rental container for a clinical study) can be cleared under temporary admission with an ATA Carnet — in that case no VAT is due, but a customs security deposit is required. We co-ordinate with Swiss forwarders and customs agents and prepare all documents in full (commercial invoice, packing list, EUR.1 movement certificate, T1). The customer receives a customs-cleared delivery, free domicile, in Zurich."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do the containers meet the Swiss energy requirement SIA 380/1?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "SIA 380/1 (thermal energy in buildings) and MuKEn 2014/2025 (Model Cantonal Energy Regulations) are stricter in Switzerland than the German Buildings Energy Act (GEG). For Zurich installations with six months or more of continuous operation we size the sandwich outer envelope at the works to U-values ≤ 0.17 W/m²K (wall and roof), triple glazing with U ≤ 1.0 W/m²K and heat recovery with an efficiency of ≥ 75 per cent. Our LABtoGO modules therefore meet the Canton of Zurich minimum requirement without retrospective insulation. Minergie or Minergie-P standards are available on request."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which electrical connection is required in Switzerland (T13/T15, 230 V/400 V)?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The Swiss low-voltage network, like the German one, operates at 230 V (single-phase) and 400 V (three-phase) at 50 Hz — electrically the installations are compatible. The sockets differ: SEV 1011 with plug type T13 (10 A, single-phase) and T15/T25 (16 A, three-phase). We fit Zurich containers at the works with T13/T15 sockets; CEE industrial sockets 16 A and 32 A are identical to Germany. The building connection is made by a Swiss electrical installer with NIN 2020 approval — we supply all schematics and wiring diagrams to Swiss standard. The connection certificate is issued by the local installer."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which applications are particularly in demand in Zurich?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Zurich is Switzerland’s second-largest pharma-biotech location after Basel. Typical uses for lab containers arise around universities (natural sciences, chemistry, biology, mechanical engineering) — BSL-2 third-party-funded containers; natural-science and medical campus sites — cleanroom and study containers; university hospitals — BSL-2 study laboratories during hospital refurbishments; pharmaceutical and diagnostics sites in the surrounding region — GMP pilot containers; and technology parks and biotech spin-offs — containers for research and small-series production."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(serviceSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(localBusinessSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbSchema)} />
