      </div>
    </section>

    <AuthorBio city="Zurich"  locale="en" />

    <!-- LABtoGO -->
    <section class="py-16 md:py-20 bg-gradient-to-br from-primary/5 via-white to-primary/10 border-y border-primary/20">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4">
        <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
          <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-10 items-center mb-10">
            <div>
              <div class="text-primary font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">Pattern interrupt · World premiere at analytica 2026</div>
              <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-heading font-bold mb-4 text-slate-900">Planexus shows Zurich what a lab container can really do in 2026</h2>
              <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">An ordinary lab container has 2,400 mm internal height. Working upright at a fume cupboard is a struggle.</p>
              <p class="text-lg text-slate-900 font-bold leading-relaxed mb-4">The Planexus LABtoGO has 2,750 mm internal height. 9.5 m external length. 3 m external width. A fully glazed front that brings genuine daylight into every Zurich research, hospital and pharmaceutical laboratory.</p>
              <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"><strong>An ordinary lab container fits entirely inside a LABtoGO.</strong> We configure it for Zurich to SIA 261 (seismic zone Z1a), SIA 380/1 (energy stricter than the German GEG), T1 customs clearance from the Albstadt works, and a T13/T15 Swiss electrical connection.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
              <div class="bg-slate-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 text-center"><div class="text-4xl font-bold text-primary mb-1">2,750</div><div class="text-xs text-gray-400 uppercase tracking-wider">mm internal height</div><div class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">Standard: 2,400 mm</div></div>
              <div class="bg-slate-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 text-center"><div class="text-4xl font-bold text-primary mb-1">9,500</div><div class="text-xs text-gray-400 uppercase tracking-wider">mm external length</div><div class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">3,000 mm width</div></div>
              <div class="bg-slate-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 text-center"><div class="text-4xl font-bold text-primary mb-1">U≤0.17</div><div class="text-xs text-gray-400 uppercase tracking-wider">W/m²K wall</div><div class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">SIA 380/1 met</div></div>
              <div class="bg-slate-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 text-center"><div class="text-4xl font-bold text-primary mb-1">SIA 261</div><div class="text-xs text-gray-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Seismic Z1a</div><div class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">structurally verified</div></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="text-center">
            <a href="/en/lab-containers/labtogo" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-primary font-bold hover:underline">View the LABtoGO platform and all configurations
              <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 12h14"/><path d="m12 5 7 7-7 7"/></svg>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PROBLEM-AGITATION -->
    <section class="py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4">
        <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
          <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-heading font-bold mb-10 text-slate-900">Planexus solves the three Zurich bottlenecks at which laboratory projects regularly fail</h2>
          <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
            <div class="bg-slate-50 border-l-4 border-primary rounded-xl p-6">
              <div class="text-slate-900 font-bold text-lg mb-2">Thayngen customs + 8.1% VAT — a bureaucratic cost trap</div>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Switzerland is not an EU customs territory. Standard freight operators invoice containers without a customs strategy — suddenly 8.1% VAT lands on the end price, T1 transit papers are missing, and daily charges accumulate at the Thayngen border. For temporary rental containers an ATA Carnet would be the right instrument — those who do not know this pay unnecessarily.</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-slate-900 font-semibold mt-3 pt-3 border-t border-gray-200">Planexus answer: T1 or ATA Carnet according to project duration, Thayngen clearance co-ordinated from the works.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-slate-50 border-l-4 border-primary rounded-xl p-6">
              <div class="text-slate-900 font-bold text-lg mb-2">SIA 261 instead of DIN — German structural calculations alone are not permittable</div>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Swiss permitting authorities do not accept DIN structural calculations on their own. SIA 261 (actions, seismic), SIA 263 (steel construction) and SIA 380/1 (energy) are binding. Containers from German works that document only DIN fail in the Zurich building-permit procedure and must be supplemented afterwards — that delays the project by 6–10 weeks.</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-slate-900 font-semibold mt-3 pt-3 border-t border-gray-200">Planexus answer: Structural design to SIA 261/263 and energy to SIA 380/1 documented from the works.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-slate-50 border-l-4 border-primary rounded-xl p-6">
              <div class="text-slate-900 font-bold text-lg mb-2">Pharma and university third-party funding — ESV instead of the German GenTSV</div>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Swiss GMP facilities are authorised by Swissmedic; genetically modified installations S1/S2/S3 go through the Einschliessungsverordnung (ESV, Containment Ordinance) at FOEN/FOPH (BAFU/BAG) — not through the German BVL/regional government. Anyone submitting GenTSV application documents in Zurich will be rejected. University third-party-funded projects require the Swiss application path from day one.</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-slate-900 font-semibold mt-3 pt-3 border-t border-gray-200">Planexus answer: ESV-compliant risk assessment, Swissmedic-ready, Swiss application documents.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- STRATEGISCHE FUSION -->
    <section class="py-16 md:py-20 bg-slate-50">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
          <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-heading font-bold mb-8 text-slate-900">Lab containers for Zurich: what German-speaking Switzerland, SIA standards and Thayngen customs clearance demand of the engineering</h2>
          <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6">
            <p><strong>Zurich is Switzerland’s largest city and, after Basel, its second-largest pharma-biotech location.</strong> Across 88 square kilometres and 12 city districts, the banking centre, universities (natural sciences, chemistry, biology, pharmacy and mechanical engineering on the Hönggerberg and Rämistrasse campuses), the natural-science and medical university campus (Campus Irchel), university hospitals (Rämistrasse), pharmaceutical and diagnostics groups in the surrounding region, and technology parks with numerous biotech spin-offs compete for space, permits and engineering capacity.</p>
            <p><strong>We know this market in detail.</strong> We know that the City of Zurich Building Permit Office at Lindenhofstrasse 19, 8001 Zurich, decides under the cantonal PBG and the ABV. We know that SIA 261 defines seismic zone Z1a for Zurich (agd = 0.6 m/s²) and that Bauwerksklasse I or II determines which safety factors apply. We know that SIA 380/1 and MuKEn 2014/2025 are stricter than the German GEG, and that for containers with six months or more of continuous operation in Zurich, U-values ≤ 0.17 W/m²K (wall and roof) and triple glazing are the standard. We know that Swiss VAT is 8.1 per cent (as at 2026), that customs clearance runs under the T1 transit procedure at the Thayngen customs office, and that rental containers can enter under temporary admission with an ATA Carnet without a VAT charge. These details are not in our quotation — they are the engineering and logistics foundation of every container that leaves our Albstadt works.</p>
            <p><strong>Made in Albstadt, customs-cleared at Thayngen, turnkey in Zurich.</strong> Final assembly and interior fit-out are carried out with Wesemann furniture in our own works — factory visits can be arranged from either side. South on the A81 via Singen, through the Thayngen border with T1 or ATA clearance, the A4 to Winterthur and the A1 to Zurich, the heavy-haulage distance is around 150 kilometres; the Zurich-Nord (A1) junctions for Hönggerberg and Irchel, Zurich-Brunau (A3) for the Rämistrasse hill and Zurich-City for Hardstrasse are standard delivery points.</p>
            <p>Whether you need a <strong>mobile laboratory in Zurich</strong> for a time-limited hospital refurbishment, a <strong>modular lab container in Zurich</strong> for a permanent university expansion at Hönggerberg, or a <strong>container laboratory in Zurich</strong> with BSL-3 containment and Swissmedic-ready GMP for a biotech spin-off in a technology park — we deliver all three answers from the same platform: the LABtoGO from our Albstadt works, with Swiss customs, structural and energy documentation from the factory.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 6 ENGINEERING-DISZIPLINEN -->
    <section class="py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4">
        <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">
          <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-heading font-bold mb-3 text-slate-900">Six engineering disciplines for German-speaking Switzerland</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-lg mb-10 max-w-3xl">Planexus builds lab containers for Zurich that are aligned in every discipline with Swiss realities — not generic, but standard- and site-specific.</p>
          <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-5">
            <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-100"><div class="text-primary text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">SIA 261 seismic</div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Z1a + building importance class</h3><p class="text-sm text-gray-600">Zurich lies in seismic zone Z1a (agd = 0.6 m/s²). We supply structural design to SIA 261 with an SIA 263 anchoring concept, BWK I or II according to risk — not DIN alone.</p></div>
            <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-100"><div class="text-primary text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">SIA 380/1 energy</div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">U ≤ 0.17 W/m²K + HR ≥ 75%</h3><p class="text-sm text-gray-600">SIA 380/1 and MuKEn 2014/2025 are stricter than the German GEG. Sandwich outer envelope, triple glazing and heat recovery sized at the works for the Swiss minimum requirement.</p></div>
            <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-100"><div class="text-primary text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Customs &amp; VAT</div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">T1 or ATA Carnet</h3><p class="text-sm text-gray-600">For permanent import, T1 transit with clearance at Thayngen (8.1% VAT). For temporary rental containers, ATA Carnet (temporary admission, no VAT) — we co-ordinate the entire process.</p></div>
            <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-100"><div class="text-primary text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">ESV &amp; Swissmedic</div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Swiss authority path, not German</h3><p class="text-sm text-gray-600">Genetically modified installations S1/S2/S3 under the Containment Ordinance (ESV) at FOEN/FOPH, GMP via Swissmedic — not GenTSV/BVL. Application documents in Swiss form.</p></div>
